                <pb facs="0591" n="495" sameAs="frus:frus1926v02/pg495"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">611.60 p 31/20: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Chargé in Latvia</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >White</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Riga</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1925-08-12T16:00:00+01:36"><hi rendition="#italic">August 12,
                                1925—4 p.m.</hi></date><lb/>[Received August 12—1:18
                        p.m.]</dateline></opener>
                <p>73. Your telegram 39, August 4, 3 p.m. Legation’s 68, August 6, 4 p.m. Having
                    orally informed Minister of Foreign Affairs of Department’s wishes for revised
                    draft of most-favored-nation <hi rendition="#italic">modus vivendi</hi> he
                    stated: (1) No agreement of the kind could take effect without ratification of
                    Diet; (2) very doubtful whether Diet would <pb facs="0592" n="496"
                        xml:id="pg496"/> ratify. Between now and November when Diet will meet he
                    considers that there is time to negotiate treaty which he is confident could be
                    ratified early in the session.</p>
                <p>By virtue of law taking effect August 8th, petroleum and products imported from
                    countries enjoying most-favored-nation treatment now obtain tariff reductions
                    which importers American oil products consider will injure their business
                    seriously.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">White</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
